Actually, I did that once -- sort of.  In Visual Studio 2003 you can 
force an app to compile to .NET bytecode.  Then you just decompile to 
the language of your choice.  The code is ugly as sin, though.  There 
was little appreciable speed difference.  That functionality has been 
removed for C code from 2005 though.

>>       don't let us stand in your way. A change to a
>> modern, platform independent language has been
>> overdue for a decade, and would make the rest of
>> this problem vanish.
> 
> Am I the only one who is worried about performance in all this discussion?
> You know, just because NetHack is a somewhat older text-interface program
> doesn't mean it isn't _huge_ and somewhat bloaty already. The memory
> requirements are already somewhat significant. When playing a longer game,
> I sometimes get a noticable delay when changing levels. Saving and loading
> savefiles can take seconds. A textbased savegame format (being the logical
> consequence of textbased bones) would multiply that. A full Java
> application with an XML parser -- I don't even want to think about it.

I am sure that it will be slower; the current code generally writes out 
the raw structs and that is about as fast as physically possible.  It is 
also the reason they are a PITA.  The $64,000 question is how much 
slower.  I won't know until I'm done and premature optimization is the 
root of all evil. :)

BTW, I wouldn't use XML even in a Java version.  XML is useful for 
interop between disparate systems and can arguably be useful in config 
files so you don't have to write your own parser, but would be (IMNSHO) 
stupid for savefiles.
> 
> NetHack is already being ported to a multitude of platforms. C _is_ more or
> less portable. Save and bones compatibility are a (minor) PITA, granted,
> but it could even be possible (although somewhat ugly) to implement a 
> portable binary format in C. Many people play NetHack on systems without
> the abundance of resources typical for modern PCs. My home machine isn't
> all that fancy, and I used to play on a decades-old laptop and a sharp
> zaurus PDA. If the next version at NH was Java only, I'd switch to another
> roguelike on the spot.
> 
If Nethack was ported to Java it would end up being a fork, not the only 
version.  I wouldn't worry about it.  I was just commenting on the fact 
that things like writing bones and save code would be much simpler and 
need less maintenace if I could interrogate the structs at runtime and 
iterate through their members instead of hardcoding them.

